      "writtenOn": "2026-03-16T10:50:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What\u0027s a difference between _AT_BOOT and _NOW protection flags in this driver.",
      "revId": "c768fb84b0673458119d52d90e855e2d801ac088",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5b2719fc_e933f411",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_flash/cros_flash_em32f967.c",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 209,
      "author": {
        "id": 1177449
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-03-18T03:50:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Since our flash protection is non-volatile, both _AT_BOOT and _NOW flags effectively represent the same protection state on our platform.\n\nIn other words, the protection is preserved across reboots, so there is no distinction between \"protection set at boot\" and \"protection active at runtime.\"",
      "parentUuid": "83989935_cfe5248c",
      "revId": "c768fb84b0673458119d52d90e855e2d801ac088",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "290f134a_d03833a7",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_flash/cros_flash_em32f967.c",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 209,
      "author": {
        "id": 1438457
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-02T13:11:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see, but it\u0027s more complicated. The flash protection is complex topic since it\u0027s super hardware depended feature and we use common flags for all SOCs.\n\nFor my understanding, the conversion comes from the SPI NOR flash chips. The [*_AT_BOOT](https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/include/ec_commands.h;l\u003d2033) flag means the flash protection is enable (Status Registers in the Flash NOR chip are set to enable flash protection). [*_NOW](https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/include/ec_commands.h;l\u003d2038) flag means the flash protection is set and can not be changed till reboot (#WP line is asserted to the flash NOR chip + some proper configuration). We are trying to mimic this with what we have. E.g. in the andes_xip driver, a flash nor xip is connected, but there is no control over #WP line. So I added a [software lock](https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/zephyr/drivers/cros_flash/cros_flash_andestech_xip.c;l\u003d107)(+ [upstream](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/main/drivers/flash/flash_andes_qspi_xip.c#L106)) in the Andes driver to block possibility of changing status registers (where the protection ranges are configured).",
      "parentUuid": "5b2719fc_e933f411",
      "revId": "c768fb84b0673458119d52d90e855e2d801ac088",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d06b76ef_cb94d18d",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_flash/cros_flash_em32f967.c",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 209,
      "author": {
        "id": 1177449
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-07T09:48:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for the clarification.\n\nIn the current implementation for EM32F967, we treat these flags with the same semantic split you described:\n\n- *_AT_BOOT reflects the protection state programmed in the flash itself\n  (derived from the protected range / status configuration, persistent across reboots).\n\n- *_NOW is reported only when there is an additional runtime mechanism preventing\n  modification of the flash protection configuration.\n\nOn our platform, this runtime condition is represented by write_protect_is_asserted(),\nso *_NOW flags are added only when write-protect is asserted. We do not assume that\n*_NOW always exists, and we avoid conflating *_AT_BOOT with *_NOW.\n\nprotect_now() therefore reuses protect_at_boot() to enforce the desired policy, while\nthe presence of *_NOW is solely determined by the runtime write-protect state, similar\nto how the Andes XIP driver models the software lock in absence of a controllable #WP pin.",
      "parentUuid": "290f134a_d03833a7",
      "revId": "c768fb84b0673458119d52d90e855e2d801ac088",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5e3f732e_b573d9d8",
        "filename": "zephyr/drivers/cros_flash/cros_flash_em32f967.c",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 209,
      "author": {
        "id": 1438457
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-08T05:59:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for the explanation. \n\nThis way, the cros-flash driver won\u0027t be able to set the _NOW flags independently from the `write_protect_is_asserted` state. In theory the \"protected now, until reboot\" state should be possible regardless of the #WP state. I\u0027m talking about  https://www.puyasemi.com/download_path/%E6%95%B0%E6%8D%AE%E6%89%8B%E5%86%8C/Flash%20%E8%8A%AF%E7%89%87/P25Q16U_Datasheet_V1.9.pdf 10.5, SRP0,SRP1 \u003d 0,1 \"Status Register is protected and cannot be written to again until the next Power-Down, Power-Up cycle\". However, I\u0027m not sure if it\u0027s even needed here. Let me think about it and let you know.",
      "parentUuid": "d06b76ef_cb94d18d",
      "revId": "c768fb84b0673458119d52d90e855e2d801ac088",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
